# Environment
NODE_ENV="development"

# CORS Security Configuration (Production - ENH-003)
# Required for production deployment to restrict API access
# Development: Leave empty/commented for open access (*)
# Production: Comma-separated list of allowed domains
# Examples:
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://your-domain.com,https://app.your-domain.com"
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://architect-platform.com,https://app.architect-platform.com"
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=""

# Content Security Policy (CSP) Configuration (Production - SEC-001)
# Controls which resources can be loaded by the browser
# Development: CSP automatically configured for Next.js compatibility
# Production: Default CSP automatically enforced with these directives:
# - default-src 'self': Only load resources from same origin
# - script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval' 'unsafe-inline': Allow Next.js inline scripts
# - style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline': Allow Tailwind CSS inline styles
# - img-src 'self' data: blob: https:: Allow images from various sources
# - frame-src 'none': Block all iframe content
# - object-src 'none': Block plugin content
# - connect-src 'self' <NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL> https://api.stripe.com: Restrict API calls
# - form-action 'self': Restrict form submissions to same origin
# - upgrade-insecure-requests: Force HTTPS
#
# Advanced CSP Configuration (Optional):
# If you need to load content from external domains (CDNs, analytics), add them to connect-src:
# Custom CSP: Set CSP_CUSTOM_DIRECTIVES environment variable for additional restrictions
# Example for external analytics: CSP_CUSTOM_DIRECTIVES="connect-src 'self' https://analytics.example.com"
#
# Security Benefits:
# - Prevents XSS attacks by blocking unauthorized scripts
# - Blocks mixed content by forcing HTTPS
# - Prevents clickjacking by blocking frames
# - Reduces attack surface by restricting resource loading

# Production Error Monitoring (Required for Enterprise Compliance)
